ated  as a check on central bank  irresponsibility and if central banks  become marginally more responsible which  the markets have kind of priced in as a  as a probability then bitcoin's gonna  gonna take a hit and that's and that's  fine and so when you think about  correlation between bitcoin and some of  these other uh nasdaq et cetera type uh  assets  is bitcoin the leading indicator or the  stocks the leading indicator like do you  have a sense for which one is leading  the other or they're just moving in  lockstep and it doesn't actually matter  they just go together  yeah funny enough i think bitcoin has  actually become more of a pure signal in  terms of like risk on risk off uh just  it seems like it's it's more of a free  market obviously uh there's there's a  lot  more capital in these equity markets and  it's like a lot of it's kind of just  auto allocated by pensions and all this  uh where they just they just need to  establish some sort of some sort of  allocation and lock it in um there's you  know tens of trillions of dollars of  assets in these equity markets where  bitcoin isn't even trillion dollar asset  and so funny enough when we saw the vix  spiking uh i believe two weeks back uh  hit like 38 and it was really bloody  monday and then the following tuesday  after uh where equities closed in the  green both times with the nasdaq down  like four and a half percent bitcoin  bought them first both of those days and  we could see like in the spot order  books that there are some huge buyers on  coinbase just happily stacking away in  in huge volumes and so what that kind of  tells me is you know the correlation is  obviously there but bitcoin is being  increasingly viewed as you know this  more pure signal of of the kind of a  check on the financial system uh and and  how unsustainable it is and so it's  almost like a leading indicator we saw  that in march of 2020 as well bitcoin  obviously is much more volatile 50 in  the day but it bottomed  two weeks before equities and it was up  40 by the time that equities ultimately  bottomed in that march uh 2020 moment so  that's kind of how i think of it going  forward is is the volatility spike done  are we going to see a liquidity crunch  in 2022 maybe and if anyone tells you  they know they they're just flying  but ultimately i think coming out of it  how the response is after some sort of  liquidity crunch uh that's where bitcoin  really takes off  got it and then when we start to think  about some of the other instruments  around bitcoin let's look at the  perpetual's funding rate uh versus kind  of like an annualized basis what are you  seeing there  yeah so uh we talked about this a lot uh  but here we actually took like an  annualized figure of it so in bitcoin  there's a couple different type of  futures instruments there's that  perpetual futures which is just a  futures contract that never expires it  just rolls over and then you have like  the quarterlies which is more in line  with like traditional commodity future  markets right like uh every three months  there's a quarterly futures expiry and  so we can take like the spread between  the current spot market and and what  that futures price is and we can have a  basis spread and so if we annualize that  we kind of get like almost a market  neutral or risk-free uh rate in the  bitcoin markets and so  uh during april that was hitting like  these really really high uh high figures  like 40 50 plus uh and those perpetual  swap markets uh basically it's kind of  this this basis between the the spot  price uh and that uh the funding  essentially keeps incentivizes uh  allocators to to remain tethered to that  spot price and so if the derivative is  trading far above the spot market  funding will be really really positive  so this is a 168 hour moving average of  that perp's funding uh and it's  annualized but basically when what you  see in in february there is that traders  were paying 100 annualized uh to long  bitcoin  right so obviously that's not  sustainable uh it kind of shows you that  these capital markets for the wild wild  west uh but for some investors it was a  really big opportunity you got paid you  know outrageous rates to just short  bitcoin and you could not even like  short it with your entire net worth you  just if you won x short bitcoin you're  holding a synthetic dollar so you could  you could harvest 50 100 annualized  yields for a week on end for just  sitting in dollar positions and so you  know we can kind of see these boom and  bust cycles and bitcoin with on chain  indicators but we can also see it uh and  kind of the sentiment cycles in the  bitcoin derivatives market and right now  uh we're basically completely muted uh  we're not we're not like severely  negative but over the last week purpose  funding has been on average negative so  what we really kind of see is it's not  derivative driven at all and actually  all kind of long speculation is has  basically been driven out over the last  two months  got it and so when you see us down uh  kind of flat do you think we have to go  super negative uh before we get any sort  of upward price movement or how do you  think about funding uh being positive or  negative in price  yeah so i mean usually in bull markets  uh  it's it's always different this time  it's different everybody longs their  longs you see the basis futures funding  it explodes upwards as everyone's just  trying to margin margin-long bitcoin  margin along crypto and during bear  markets you saw the same thing at the  bottom of the 2018 cycle um everybody  thinks bitcoin's dead it's all over you  know they're longing the absolute or  they're shorting the absolute bottom and  you see that like the perps market  drives far below the spot market and so  it's almost like uh their derivatives  are trying to like push that volleyball  underwater that's how i describe it  they're pushing a beach ball underwater  um we're naturally th